We are given the values of the polynomial $p(x)$ at $x=2,3,4,5,6,7$:
\begin{align*} p(2) &= \frac{2}{2^2-1} = \frac{2}{3}, \\ p(3) &= \frac{3}{3^2-1} = \frac{3}{8}, \\ p(4) &= \frac{4}{4^2-1} = \frac{4}{15}, \\ p(5) &= \frac{5}{5^2-1} = \frac{5}{24}, \\ p(6) &= \frac{6}{6^2-1} = \frac{6}{35}, \\ p(7) &= \frac{7}{7^2-1} = \frac{7}{48}. \end{align*}
To find $p(8),$ we can use Lagrange interpolation. The Lagrange interpolation formula for a polynomial $p(x)$ of degree $n$ is:
\[ p(x) = \sum_{i=0}^{n} y_i \prod_{j \neq i} \frac{x-x_j}{x_i-x_j}, \]
where $(x_0,y_0), (x_1,y_1), \dots, (x_n,y_n)$ are the given points. In this case, $n=6$ and the points are $(2,\frac{2}{3}), (3,\frac{3}{8}), (4,\frac{4}{15}), (5,\frac{5}{24}), (6,\frac{6}{35}), (7,\frac{7}{48}).$ Thus,
\begin{align*} p(8) &= \frac{2}{3} \prod_{j=3}^{7} \frac{8-j}{2-j} + \frac{3}{8} \prod_{j=2}^{6} \frac{8-j}{3-j} + \frac{4}{15} \prod_{j=2}^{5} \frac{8-j}{4-j} + \frac{5}{24} \prod_{j=2}^{4} \frac{8-j}{5-j} + \frac{6}{35} \prod_{j=2}^{3} \frac{8-j}{6-j} + \frac{7}{48} \prod_{j=2}^{2} \frac{8-j}{7-j} \\ &= \frac{2}{3} \cdot \frac{(8-3)(8-4)(8-5)(8-6)(8-7)}{(2-3)(2-4)(2-5)(2-6)(2-7)} + \frac{3}{8} \cdot \frac{(8-2)(8-3)(8-4)(8-5)(8-6)}{(3-2)(3-4)(3-5)(3-6)(3-7)} + \frac{4}{15} \cdot \frac{(8-2)(8-3)(8-4)(8-5)}{(4-2)(4-3)(4-5)(4-6)} + \frac{5}{24} \cdot \frac{(8-2)(8-3)(8-4)}{(5-2)(5-3)(5-4)} + \frac{6}{35} \cdot \frac{(8-2)(8-3)}{(6-2)(6-3)} + \frac{7}{48} \cdot \frac{(8-2)}{(7-2)}. \end{align*}
Simplifying each term, we get:
\begin{align*} p(8) &= \frac{2}{3} \cdot \frac{5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot (-1)}{(-1) \cdot (-2) \cdot (-3) \cdot (-4) \cdot (-5)} + \frac{3}{8} \cdot \frac{6 \cdot 5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2}{1 \cdot (-1) \cdot (-2) \cdot (-3) \cdot (-4)} + \frac{4}{15} \cdot \frac{6 \cdot 5 \cdot 4}{2 \cdot (-1) \cdot (-2)} + \frac{5}{24} \cdot \frac{6 \cdot 5 \cdot 4}{3 \cdot (-1) \cdot (-2)} + \frac{6}{35} \cdot \frac{6 \cdot 5}{4 \cdot 3} + \frac{7}{48} \cdot \frac{6}{5}. \end{align*}
Calculating each term, we have:
\begin{align*} p(8) &= \frac{2}{3} \cdot \frac{-120}{-60} + \frac{3}{8} \cdot \frac{360}{12} + \frac{4}{15} \cdot \frac{120}{-4} + \frac{5}{24} \cdot \frac{120}{-6} + \frac{6}{35} \cdot \frac{30}{12} + \frac{7}{48} \cdot \frac{6}{5} \\ &= \frac{2}{3} \cdot 2 + \frac{3}{8} \cdot 30 + \frac{4}{15} \cdot (-30) + \frac{5}{24} \cdot (-20) + \frac{6}{35} \cdot \frac{5}{2} + \frac{7}{48} \cdot \frac{6}{5} \\ &= \frac{4}{3} + \frac{90}{8} - \frac{120}{15} - \frac{100}{24} + \frac{30}{35} + \frac{42}{240} \\ &= \frac{4}{3} + \frac{45}{4} - 8 - \frac{25}{6} + \frac{6}{7} + \frac{7}{40}. \end{align*}
Finding a common denominator and simplifying, we get:
\begin{align*} p(8) &= \frac{4 \cdot 80 + 45 \cdot 20 - 8 \cdot 120 - 25 \cdot 10 + 6 \cdot 14 + 7 \cdot 3}{240} \\ &= \frac{320 + 900 - 960 - 250 + 84 + 21}{240} \\ &= \frac{171}{240} \\ &= \frac{57}{80}. \end{align*}Therefore, $p(8) = \boxed{\frac{57}{80}}$.The answer is: \frac{57}{80}
[Final answer] \frac{57}{80}

